---
// Fall week 1: teams are announced and sit together for the first time.
// Five minutes of information, the Two truths and a bug rules and the
// instructor's own round (a placeholder slide the instructor fills before
// class), fifteen minutes to find the team on the seating map, fifteen to
// play, then the Team Charter kickoff for the rest of the session. Served
// at /decks/fall-week-1/. The seating map is <SeatingMap>, drawn at build
// time from the room in src/lib/seating.mjs. A line credited
// to a handbook page uses that page's words; the charter is linked, not
// retyped.
import Notes from "../components/deck/Notes.astro";
import QrSlide from "../components/deck/QrSlide.astro";
import SeatingMap from "../components/deck/SeatingMap.astro";
import Slide from "../components/deck/Slide.astro";
import StatementSlide from "../components/deck/StatementSlide.astro";
import TermSessions from "../components/deck/TermSessions.astro";
import TitleSlide from "../components/deck/TitleSlide.astro";
import TwoColumnSlide from "../components/deck/TwoColumnSlide.astro";

export const title = "Team Introductions and Charter";

const charter = new URL("/assignments/team-charter/", Astro.site).href;
---

<TitleSlide
  kicker="CS 461, fall week 1"
  title="Team Introductions and Charter"
  subtitle="Meet your team, then start the agreement you'll work under"
  byline="Kirsten Winters and Alex Ulbrich, your instructors"
>
  <Notes time="0:15" cue="Keep the information to five minutes; the room moves next.">
    <p>Five minutes of information, then teams find their seats.</p>
  </Notes>
</TitleSlide>

<Slide kicker="Today" title="Agenda">
  <ol>
    <li>Information from the instruction team</li>
    <li>Icebreaker: Two truths and a bug, with the instructor's round first</li>
    <li>Find your team, sit together, and play</li>
    <li>Start the Team Charter, and ask questions</li>
  </ol>
  <Notes time="0:30" cue="Information is five minutes; keep to it.">
    <p>
      The budget: 5 minutes of information, about 4 for the icebreaker rules
      and the instructor's round, 15 to find your team, 15 to play (wants
      included), then the charter for the rest of the session.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="This week" title="Email your project partner">
  <ul>
    <li>By the end of week 1, send the introduction email to your project partner, or to your mentor, with every teammate on Cc</li>
    <li class="fragment">Start from the introduction email template, which already carries the questions worth asking before you meet</li>
    <li class="fragment">Send it as soon as you know your team: the meeting you're asking for has to fit somebody else's calendar</li>
    <li class="fragment">If every teammate already meets with them, don't send it: message your instructors at cs-46x-help@oregonstate.edu by the end of week 1</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:15" cue="Send it today if you can.">
    <p>
      All four lines are from Term Startup, which links the template. Your
      partner's contact details arrive by email at 11 AM, as the slide after
      next says.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Due next" title="Weeks 1 and 2" wide>
  <TermSessions term="fall" from={1} to={2} rows={["Due", "In class"]} />
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Point at week 2: the charter is due then.">
    <p>
      From the schedule. The fall week 2 check-in goes through your Term
      Startup list while it is still in progress.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="After this session" title="Your partner, your TA, and shared projects">
  <ul>
    <li>Your project partner's contact details arrive by email at 11 AM</li>
    <li class="fragment">Your team's TA is assigned soon, and reaches out to your team</li>
    <li class="fragment">Some projects run more than one team: those teams agree with their partner how to split the work</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:15" cue="Ask who is on a project with more than one team.">
    <p>
      TA check-ins are at a time your team and your TA agree. For Students:
      what a team owns on a shared project can be an interface, a
      repository, or a milestone, and each team writes its part into its
      Definition of Shipped.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Icebreaker, fifteen minutes" title="Two truths and a bug">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Write three claims each:</strong> two true and one invented, all about your life with computers</li>
    <li class="fragment"><strong>Guess before the reveal:</strong> the team votes on which claim is the lie, then the author says. One round per person, a minute each, no deliberating</li>
    <li class="fragment"><strong>Chase the interesting one:</strong> whichever claim caused the most argument, ask for the story behind it</li>
    <li class="fragment"><strong>End with a want:</strong> one sentence each on what you most want to be better at by the time this project ends</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Explain the rules here; the instructor's round on the next slide shows them.">
    <p>
      Teams play once they're seated, after the map. Specific beats
      impressive: "I took down production with a missing semicolon", "I
      learned to program on a graphing calculator", "I have never once used a
      debugger". Nobody collects the wants.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Two truths and a bug: the instructor's round" title="Which one is the bug?">
  <div class="deck-placeholder">
    <p class="deck-placeholder-label">Placeholder: the instructor's three claims</p>
    <ol>
      <li>[First claim]</li>
      <li>[Second claim]</li>
      <li>[Third claim]</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <Notes time="2:00" cue="Read all three, take a show of hands on each, then say which is the bug.">
    <p>
      Placeholder: replace the three bracketed claims with your own two
      truths and a bug, about your life with computers, before class, and
      remove the dashed box. One round for the whole room shows the pace the
      teams play at: a minute, a vote, the reveal, and a question about the
      claim that caused the most argument.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<StatementSlide kicker="Teams are announced">
  Find your team number on the map, and sit together.
  <Notes time="0:15" cue="Advance to the map as soon as this is read.">
    <p>Next slide is the map. Leave it up while people move.</p>
  </Notes>
</StatementSlide>

<SeatingMap teams={65}>
  <Notes time="30:00" cue="Minute 15: teams start the icebreaker. Minute 27: call for wants.">
    <p>
      The seating map. Left and right are as the room faces the screen.
      Fifteen minutes to find seats and say hello, then fifteen for Two
      truths and a bug at the table, wants included, with the map still up.
      Point a lost team to its section's range label first, then its number.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</SeatingMap>

<TwoColumnSlide kicker="Team Charter kickoff" title="What the Team Charter page requires" divider>
  <div slot="left">
    <h3>Required Charter Sections</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Roles</li>
      <li>Decision Process</li>
      <li>Cadence and Tools</li>
      <li>Conflict and Accountability</li>
      <li>Inclusion Norms</li>
      <li>AI and Confidentiality</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <div slot="right">
    <h3>Required in <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code></h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Definition of Done</li>
      <li>The Contribution Norm</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <Notes time="2:00" cue="Teams open the page on the next slide and start writing.">
    <p>
      These are the page's own section headings; the page says what each one
      needs and holds the rubric. Read it as a team before writing.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TwoColumnSlide>

<QrSlide kicker="Start now" title="The Team Charter page" url={charter}>
  <p>Every required section, and the rubric it's scored on.</p>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="Teams work on the charter for the rest of the session.">
    <p>Walk the room; the instruction team takes questions table by table.</p>
  </Notes>
</QrSlide>
